'''COLUMN: NINETY SECONDS — by Wakita Hiroko, Editor-in-Chief'''

Eleven days of silence. Ninety seconds of statement. That is the ratio. Eleven days of silence. Ninety seconds of statement. The Foreign Ministry has spoken — but barely.

The FM did not appear. The FM sent a 34-year-old deputy director who has never handled a press conference. The statement lasted 90 seconds. No questions were taken. The statement addressed the Castellano — shipping company identified, request to Balboa for corporate records, review of Zong Trade Bridge, FM press conference tomorrow. The statement did NOT address the Ayorin attacks. The statement did NOT address the eleven days of silence. The statement did NOT address the diplomatic cascade — Burawa, Asikyira, Hersatia.

The drugs are a crisis. The attacks are a crisis. The Foreign Ministry addressed one crisis and ignored the other. The FM will hold a full press conference tomorrow. The Okami Times will be there. The questions will not be 90 seconds.

The first mate is talking. The Sansi ambassador is talking. The Zong ambassador is talking — conditionally. The community is talking. The press corps is talking. The Foreign Ministry is not talking. The Foreign Ministry is reading. There is a difference. The Foreign Ministry will learn it tomorrow.

The Okami Times asks: why did it take eleven days? Why did it take 850kg of drugs? Why did the Foreign Ministry mention the drugs and not the attacks? Why did the Foreign Ministry send a junior spokesperson to read a statement that the Foreign Minister should have read eleven days ago?

The Ayorin community responded today. Asikyira cultural association chair Burawa Yuki: "The Foreign Ministry mentioned the drugs. The Foreign Ministry did not mention us." The community spoke for three hours. The Foreign Ministry spoke for 90 seconds. The community said more. The community has been saying more for eleven days.

'''SHINTENCHI-DORO — The People Save What the Government Would Not'''
The DSA exemption motion failed. 241-232. Nine votes. The government could not save Tsubaki-ya. The community did.

5,400 donors. ¥2.3M in 12 hours. The Shintenchi-doro Merchants' Association organised the fund. The community saved Tsubaki-ya. The excess ¥500K will establish a permanent relief fund for small businesses facing DSA compliance costs.

The Okami Times notes: the community saved Tsubaki-ya because the government would not. The DSA exemption motion failed by nine votes. Nine votes separated a small business from closure. The Shintenchi-doro Relief Fund is a model — but it should not be necessary. The DSA must be amended. Small businesses are not large systems. The government created the burden. The community created the solution. Both are true. Both are Okaiken. But only one should be necessary.

